NEARS-2301 delta re-QA [8] — AC4 — environment blocker evidence
=================================================================

Claim: VendorApp's Android baseUrl cannot be pointed at the worktree-scoped
QA backend (127.0.0.1:8011, code with the NEARS-2301 fix) without editing
product code, and `adb reverse` does not redirect it.

1. Source confirmed hardcoded, no override:
   VendorApp/lib/util/app_constants.dart:14
     static String get baseUrl => GetPlatform.isIOS ? 'http://127.0.0.1:8000' : 'http://10.0.2.2:8000';
   No --dart-define/String.fromEnvironment usage anywhere in VendorApp/lib.
   VendorApp/lib/helper/get_di.dart:179
     Get.lazyPut(() => ApiClient(appBaseUrl: AppConstants.baseUrl, sharedPreferences: Get.find()));

2. `adb -s emulator-5556 reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8011` was set BEFORE launching
   VendorApp (flutter run -d emulator-5556 from this worktree, pid 42570).

3. Empirical proof the reverse rule had no effect on 10.0.2.2 traffic:
   - Worktree backend request log (port 8011, this worktree's own
     `php artisan serve`, pid 9205) — last line before app launch was
     2026-08-23 15:06:05; it recorded ZERO new lines through app boot,
     login, and full DM list->preview->edit->validate flow (checked
     repeatedly through 15:27:31).
   - Primary-tree backend log (port 8000, pid 28927, run from
     /Users/Apple/Projects/nears/Admin — NOT this worktree, does NOT carry
     the NEARS-2301 backend fix — grep confirmed `OWNER_VISIBLE_ATTRIBUTES`
     absent from that tree's DeliveryMan.php) grew continuously throughout,
     including bursts exactly aligned with every VendorApp action taken in
     this session (e.g. 15:09:4x-15:10:15 at app boot).
   - Admin/storage/logs/laravel.log for the PRIMARY tree grew from
     78542085 -> 78549057 bytes with entries timestamped 15:15:34, seconds
     before the check at 15:15:52 — proving the shared port-8000 backend is
     under live, active, concurrent use by another session RIGHT NOW, so it
     could not safely be paused/commandeered to test this fix either.

Conclusion: no code-free, DB-safe path exists in this environment to route
VendorApp's live network traffic to the worktree's patched backend. The
null-safety code fix itself (add_delivery_man_screen.dart:57) was verified:
(a) statically — reads _deliveryMan!.email ?? '' exactly as the ticket
    describes;
(b) via the engineer's own widget test
    VendorApp/test/features/deliveryman/add_delivery_man_screen_null_email_test.dart
    test (a) "null email (post-backend-fix payload shape) does not throw,
    field renders empty" — PASSED;
(c) partially live: edit screen opens without crash against a REAL DM
    record (store 35 / DM id 6, populated-email case — the pre-fix shape),
    the empty-email client-side validation snackbar
    ("Enter delivery man email address") fires live with ZERO network call
    dispatched (confirmed against both backend logs, no new line at the
    action's timestamp) — so no shared-DB mutation occurred.

Recommended follow-up: add a `--dart-define`/env-based baseUrl override to
VendorApp/UserApp/DeliveryApp's app_constants.dart so a worktree-isolated
backend (the documented "own php artisan serve --port=<n> + DB copy"
pattern) can actually be reached by a live Android/iOS build in future QA
passes that need both a private DB AND a real device. Without it, any
future ticket combining a backend contract change with a live-device
frontend regression check will hit this exact same blocker.
